# Workspace Venv Fallback

RULE: If a repo's local venv (e.g. `.venv`) isn't working — missing deps, wrong Python, import errors — using the workspace venv is fine.

WHY: Repo venvs can be out of sync or missing transitive deps (e.g. google-cloud-pubsub, unified-feature-calculator). The workspace venv (`.venv-workspace`) is shared and typically has all deps installed.

## Workspace venv name

The workspace venv is always named `.venv-workspace` across workspaces. Use:

```bash
source .venv-workspace/bin/activate
```

or from workspace root:

```bash
cd $WORKSPACE_ROOT && source .venv-workspace/bin/activate
```

## When to use workspace venv

- Repo `.venv` has `ModuleNotFoundError` for transitive deps
- Repo `.venv` uses wrong Python version
- Quality gates or tests fail due to env gaps in repo venv
- Running cross-repo commands (e.g. features-delta-one-service + features-calendar-service)

## DO

- Use workspace venv for pytest, quality gates, and imports when repo venv fails
- Document in prompts: "run from WORKSPACE_ROOT with source .venv-workspace/bin/activate"

## NEVER

- Assume repo venv is always correct; workspace venv is a valid fallback
- Block on repo venv issues when workspace venv works
